          explanation:
            '<p>If a slightly soluble salt is dissolved in a solution that already contains one of its ions (a <strong>common ion</strong>), its solubility is <em>reduced</em> compared to dissolving in pure water.</p>' +
            '<p>For example, dissolving PbCl<sub>2</sub> in a 0.10 M NaCl solution: Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> is already present. By Le Chatelier\'s principle, the extra Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> shifts the dissolution equilibrium <em>left</em>, reducing the amount of PbCl<sub>2</sub> that dissolves.</p>' +
            '<p>Mathematically, the K<sub>sp</sub> expression still applies, but the initial [Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>] is no longer zero — it starts at the common-ion concentration.</p>',
